.TH PRINTBIGINT 3 "2026-09-24" "BigNums" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
printBigInt, printBigFloat \- print a big number in decimal to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqbignums.h\(dq
.sp
.BI "void printBigInt(const BigInt *" "a" ");"
.sp
.BI "void printBigFloat(const BigFloat *" "x" ","
.BI "    int " "decimal_places" ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B printBigInt()
writes the unsigned integer a in decimal to stdout.
.B printBigFloat()
writes x with a sign if negative, an integer part, a decimal point, and
fractional digits.  Negative decimal_places is treated as zero.
Even zero decimal places prints a decimal point: exact 2 prints "2.".
The float is rounded to nearest at the requested decimal place, with ties
away from zero.  Rounding carries into the integer part when necessary.
.PP
Neither function modifies its input or appends a newline.
The caller may use putchar to terminate a line.
.PP
Decimal digit buffers are sized from MAX_LIMBS for both integer and
fractional conversion.  The largest 32768-bit unsigned integer has 9865
decimal digits and can be printed in full by printBigInt().
.SH RETURN VALUE
These functions return no value.  Check stdout for stream errors.
printBigFloat() sets errno to ERANGE and writes nothing if decimal_places
exceeds MAX_LIMBS * 10 (10240), or if the integer part requires more than
32768 bits.  Include <errno.h> to inspect this error.
Set errno to zero before calling printBigFloat() to distinguish a new range
error from a previously stored value; success does not clear errno.
.SH NOTES
A negative nonzero value that rounds to zero retains its minus sign.
.PP
Formatting rounds the stored binary value exactly; it cannot restore
precision already lost during earlier arithmetic.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bignums (7)
